====================================================================
DOMAIN: Humanitat, Providence, and the Normative Telos of History
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-09
PAPERS_FOUND: 15 total (High: 7, Medium: 7, Low: 1)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The secondary literature on Herder's Humanitat clusters around three overlapping debates. The first concerns the conceptual status of Humanitat itself: is it a fixed essence, a developmental ideal achieved through Bildung, or a regulative horizon that motivates but never fully determines historical development? Sikka (2011) argues for a "qualified relativism" in which Humanitat combines a universal conception of human nature with recognition that its expression is irreducibly shaped by cultural particularity. Barnard (2003) stresses the tension between the universal and the national, showing how Herder's insistence on incommensurable cultural distinctiveness sits uneasily with his call for a common humanity. Bristow (2025) and James (2022) both read Humanitat as the telos of a universal history that is genuinely philosophical -- not merely descriptive -- yet open-ended and non-triumphalist.

The second debate concerns Herder's theology and its relation to his philosophy of history. His 1787 Gott adopts a modified Spinozism in which God and nature are identified through the concept of Kraft (active force), replacing Spinoza's static substance with a dynamic, teleological power pervading all things. Hampton (2015) shows that this revision drew on Ralph Cudworth's "plastic nature," mediating between transcendent reason and mechanism. Forster (2012) argues that Herder's Spinozism is not a simple adoption but a transformation that introduces genuine developmental teleology absent from Spinoza. Solanki (2024) shows how this Spinozist-inflected analogical thinking grounds Herder's comparative method for both nature and culture. Crowe (2012) demonstrates that Herder's perfectionist ethics is underwritten by this theistic framework: the moral telos of Humanitat is embedded in a providential order, not merely a human aspiration.

The third debate concerns the consistency of Herder's optimism about progress with his critique of progressive historiography. Wells (1960) identifies two philosophies of history in Herder -- one relativist (Auch eine Philosophie), one progressivist (Ideen) -- and argues they cannot be fully reconciled. Denby (2005) contests this, showing that Herder's critique of Enlightenment progress is itself unstable: his concept of Kultur operates as a quasi-Enlightenment category. Bristow (2025) proposes that both the incomparability thesis and the history thesis are reconciled in Herder's practice of universal history as a philosophical inquiry that is empirical, open-ended, and skeptical. Bollacher (2017) shows how Herder's "globe ontology" rejects hierarchically structured history while retaining Humanitat as an embedded telos.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain is central to the research project. The concept of Humanitat is Herder's primary normative category, and its meaning determines the coherence of his philosophy of history as a whole: how providence, progress, cultural particularity, and moral value interrelate depends on what Humanitat is and whether it can do the normative work Herder assigns to it. The tensions identified in this domain -- between telos and particularity, between providentialism and pluralism, between critique of progress and commitment to universal history -- are the core intellectual problems of the research project.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The specifically theological dimension of Herder's providentialism in the Ideen (as distinct from the Spinozist metaphysics of Gott) is underexplored: most scholarship treats providence either as vestigial theology or as Spinozist naturalism, without attending to its specific function in structuring historical narrative. The reception of Herder's Briefe zur Beförderung der Humanität as a late-period revision of the Humanitat concept is also relatively underexamined in English-language scholarship.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The debate between Wells's two-philosophies thesis and Bristow's reconciliation via universal history provides a useful structuring tension for synthesis. The Spinozism debate (Hampton, Forster, Solanki) should be connected to the question of whether Herder's providentialism is theistically or naturalistically grounded.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Qualified relativism (Humanitat as universal but culturally expressed): 3 papers - Sikka, Denby, Barnard
- Teleological universalism (Humanitat as telos of universal history): 3 papers - Bristow, James, Bollacher
- Spinozist/theological grounding: 3 papers - Hampton, Forster, Solanki
- Perfectionist ethics with theistic underpinning: 1 paper - Crowe
- Tension between two philosophies of history: 2 papers - Wells, Zammito
====================================================================
}

@book{sikka2011herder,
  author = {Sikka, Sonia},
  title = {Herder on Humanity and Cultural Difference: Enlightened Relativism},
  year = {2011},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511783012},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Sikka argues that Herder's position on culture and humanity is a "qualified relativism" -- a sophisticated blend of universalism and relativism rather than outright cultural relativism. Herder presupposes a common human nature and a set of basic human goods that ground transcultural ethical judgments, while insisting that the realization of these goods is always culturally shaped and that individuals can only be evaluated within their own cultural context.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses what Humanitat is: Sikka's argument that Humanitat combines a universal conception of human nature with recognition of its culturally particular expressions is the most systematic treatment of the concept available in English. The book's account of Herder's anthropology, metaphysics, ethics, and philosophy of history makes it the essential secondary source for understanding the normative telos problem.

  POSITION: Defends qualified relativism against both simple universalist and simple relativist readings of Herder.
  },
  keywords = {Humanitat, cultural-relativism, universalism, philosophy-of-history, High},
  abstract = {Herder is often criticized for having embraced cultural relativism, but there has been little philosophical discussion of what he actually wrote about the nature of the human species and its differentiation through culture. This book focuses on Herder's idea of culture, seeking to situate his social and political theses within the context of his anthropology, metaphysics, epistemology, ethics, theory of language and philosophy of history. It argues for a view of Herder as a qualified relativist, who combined the conception of a common human nature with a belief in the importance of culture in developing and shaping that nature. Especially highlighted are Herder's understanding of the relativity of virtue and happiness, and his belief in the impossibility of constructing a single best society. The book will appeal to a wide range of readers interested both in Herder and in Enlightenment culture more generally.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{barnard2003herder,
  author = {Barnard, F.M.},
  title = {Herder on Nationality, Humanity, and History},
  year = {2003},
  publisher = {McGill-Queen's University Press},
  doi = {10.1515/9780773570917},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Barnard argues that Herder's core philosophical commitment is to embed human creativity in particular cultural and linguistic communities, while simultaneously requiring integration of cultural particularity into a broader sense of universal humanity. He shows that Herder recognized the dangers of ethnic fanaticism and what we now call globalization, seeking to balance communitarian and cosmopolitan dimensions of public life through the concept of Humanitat.

  RELEVANCE: Barnard is the foremost English-language interpreter of Herder's political and social philosophy. This book directly addresses the tension between cultural particularity and normative universalism -- the core question of whether Herder's telos of Humanitat can coexist with the irreducible worth of individual cultures. Barnard's earlier 1965 book is the standard political reading; this later work updates it with the humanity/history nexus.

  POSITION: Stresses the productive tension between nationality and humanity, reading Herder as a pluralist who requires both.
  },
  keywords = {Humanitat, nationality, humanity, history, universalism, High},
  abstract = {The core of J.G. Herder's philosophy of nationalism lies in the conviction that human creativity must be embedded in the particular culture of a communal language. While he acknowledged that this cultural particular must be integrated into a more universal humanity, he insisted that each culture should preserve its incommensurable distinctiveness. He also called for a new method of enquiry regarding history, one that demands empathetic sensitivity toward the uniquely individual while realizing that there are few gains without losses.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{bristow2025herder,
  author = {Bristow, William},
  title = {Herder, Philosophy and Universal History},
  journal = {The European Legacy},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {30},
  number = {6},
  pages = {712--727},
  doi = {10.1080/10848770.2025.2525597},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Bristow proposes that the tension between Herder's incomparability thesis (each people must be understood in its own uniqueness) and his history thesis (peoples are parts of a universal history of humanity) is resolved through Herder's distinctive practice of universal history: an empirical, critical, skeptical, and open-ended philosophical inquiry into the meaning and telos of human existence. Bristow compares this project to Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit and argues that it constitutes a third major Herderian contribution to European thought beyond language philosophy and cultural anthropology.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses whether a universal normative telos (Humanitat) can coexist with the irreducible particularity of cultures -- one of the key research questions. Bristow's reading of Herder's universal history as genuinely philosophical and open-ended is the most recent treatment of the problem and offers a constructive reconciliation.

  POSITION: Reconciliationist: the tension between incomparability and universal history is resolved through the philosophical character of Herder's historical inquiry itself.
  },
  keywords = {universal-history, Humanitat, telos, incomparability, Hegel, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{james2022situating,
  author = {James, David},
  title = {Situating the Enlightenment in {H}erder's Philosophy of History},
  journal = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {16},
  number = {3},
  pages = {247--270},
  doi = {10.1163/18722636-12341480},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: James argues that despite his criticisms of Enlightenment abstraction and hostility to prejudice, Herder's philosophy of history commits him to the view that the Enlightenment age makes a distinctive contribution to the development of humanity -- but one that cannot be ranked above earlier epochs because each epoch has equal status by virtue of its specific contribution to the full development of humanity.

  RELEVANCE: Directly engages the tension between Herder's critique of progress narratives and his retention of a universal telos of Humanitat. James shows how the equal worth of epochs is compatible with their making distinct qualitative contributions, which is essential for understanding how Herder's providentialism functions without collapsing into a simple progress narrative.

  POSITION: Argues for a pluralist teleology in which each epoch has equal standing yet contributes distinctively to universal human development.
  },
  keywords = {Enlightenment, philosophy-of-history, equal-worth-of-epochs, Humanitat, High},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
 Although Herder is critical of the Enlightenment, I show that his philosophy of history commits him to the claim that the age and culture shaped by the Enlightenment in some way makes a distinctive contribution to the development of humanity. Yet this contribution cannot make this age and culture superior to earlier ones, for this would violate Herder’s commitment to the principle that each age and culture ought to be accorded an equal status because of the equal value of its contribution to the full development of humanity. I argue that an abstract way of thinking and a hostility to prejudice are integral elements of Herder’s attempt to situate the Enlightenment in his philosophy of history, despite how he otherwise criticizes the Enlightenment for its abstract thinking and its hostility to prejudice.},
}

@article{crowe2012herder,
  author = {Crowe, Benjamin D.},
  title = {Herder's Moral Philosophy: Perfectionism, Sentimentalism and Theism},
  journal = {British Journal for the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {20},
  number = {6},
  pages = {1141--1161},
  doi = {10.1080/09608788.2012.731243},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Crowe argues that Herder's moral philosophy combines three elements -- perfectionism (the idea that human beings have a nature that is to be developed), sentimentalism (moral motivation through feeling and sympathy), and theism (the perfectionist telos is grounded in God's providential order). The theological element is not incidental but constitutive: Herder's perfectionism requires a divine framework to give the developmental ideal its normative force and to explain why human nature tends toward Humanitat.

  RELEVANCE: This article is essential for understanding how Herder's theology connects to his normative philosophy of history. Crowe's demonstration that perfectionism requires theism directly addresses the question of whether Herder's Gott-period Spinozism underwrites or undermines the providential structure of the Ideen. It also clarifies what kind of telos Humanitat is: not a free-floating regulative ideal but a theistically grounded perfectionist end.

  POSITION: Defends a theistic-perfectionist reading of Herder's ethics against purely secular or naturalistic interpretations.
  },
  keywords = {moral-philosophy, perfectionism, theism, providence, Humanitat, High},
  abstract = {While the last several decades have seen a renaissance of scholarship on J. G. Herder (1744–1804), his moral philosophy has not been carefully examined. The aim of this paper is to fill this gap, and to point the way for further research, by reconstructing his original and systematically articulated views on morality. Three interrelated elements of his position are explored in detail: (1) his perfectionism, or theory of the human good; (2) his sentimentalism, which includes moral epistemology and a theory of moral education; and (3) his theism, which deepens and justifies these other elements.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{hampton2015english,
  author = {Hampton, Alexander J. B.},
  title = {An English Source of German Romanticism: {H}erder's {C}udworth Inspired Revision of {S}pinoza from {`P}lastik{'} to {`K}raft{'}},
  journal = {The Heythrop Journal},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {58},
  number = {3},
  pages = {417--431},
  doi = {10.1111/heyj.12272},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Hampton demonstrates that Herder's revision of Spinoza in Gott: Einige Gesprache (1787) drew on Ralph Cudworth's concept of "plastic nature" -- a middle principle mediating between transcendent divine reason and mechanical materialism. Herder replaced Spinoza's static substance-monism with the dynamic principle of Kraft (active force), transforming a deterministic pantheism into a teleological naturalism. This is identified as a significant but overlooked English contribution to the German intellectual tradition.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the character of Herder's Spinozism in Gott and how it was modified to accommodate teleological dynamism. The transformation from Spinoza's static God to Herder's Kraft-based God is crucial for understanding whether Herder's providentialism in the philosophy of history is genuinely teleological or merely nominally so. This article explains why Herder's modified Spinozism can support providential history in a way that Spinoza's original system cannot.

  POSITION: Stresses the un-Spinozist (Cudworthian) elements in Herder's appropriation of Spinoza, reading it as a creative revision rather than simple adoption.
  },
  keywords = {Spinozism, Kraft, Gott, Cudworth, pantheism, pantheism-controversy, High},
  abstract = {This examination considers the influence of the seventeenth century Cambridge Platonist Cudworth upon the thought of the late eighteenth century German thinker Herder. It focuses upon Herder's use of Cudworth's philosophy to create a revised version of Spinoza's metaphysics. Both Cudworth and Herder were concerned with the problem of determinism. Cudworth outlined a number of difficulties relating to this problem in the thought of Spinoza and proposed amendments, particularly the introduction of the middle principle of plastik , which would mediate between the Ideas of transcendent reason and mechanical materialism. We find these amendments to Spinoza's philosophy also employed in Herder's contribution to the Pantheism Controversy, in which he too offers a revised Spinozism and introduces his own middle principle of Kraft . This demonstrates an important but under‐explored English contribution to a key development in German intellectual history. The Pantheism Controversy was an epoch‐making event, helping to bring an end to the German Enlightenment and to inaugurate the Romantic movement. Herder's version of Spinoza's thought revived the philosopher's fortunes, and Herder's notion of Kraft became central to Romantic aesthetics. Finally, Herder's use of Cudworth demonstrates the important but overlooked source of Platonic realism in German Romantic thought.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{forster2012herder,
  author = {Forster, Michael N.},
  title = {Herder and {S}pinoza},
  booktitle = {Spinoza and German Idealism},
  editor = {F{\"o}rster, Eckart and Melamed, Yitzhak Y.},
  year = {2012},
  pages = {59--84},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9781139135139.005},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Forster argues that the flourishing of Spinozism in late eighteenth-century Germany owes more to Herder than to Jacobi or Mendelssohn, whose famous Pantheismusstreit debate is usually credited. Herder's version of Spinoza introduces genuine developmental and teleological elements absent from Spinoza himself, making Herder's "Spinozism" a transformation that enables rather than precludes a philosophy of historical development grounded in divine immanence.

  RELEVANCE: Establishes the precise character of Herder's engagement with Spinoza in the context of the Pantheism Controversy, which is background for understanding how Gott relates to the Ideen. Forster's argument that Herder's Spinozism incorporates teleology directly addresses the question of how the theological dimension informs Herder's providentialist philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Argues for Herder's creative and transformative (not merely derivative) use of Spinoza, stressing the developmental and teleological innovations.
  },
  keywords = {Spinozism, Gott, Pantheism-Controversy, German-idealism, providence, High},
  abstract = {A summary is not available for this content so a preview has been provided. Please use the Get access link above for information on how to access this content.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{nassar2015analogy,
  author = {Nassar, Dalia},
  title = {Analogy, Natural History and the Philosophy of Nature},
  journal = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {9},
  number = {2},
  pages = {240--257},
  doi = {10.1163/18722636-12341302},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Nassar examines Kant's 1785 critical reviews of Herder's Ideen, showing that Kant's central objection is not to Herder's content but to his methodology: Herder's reliance on analogy (treating human and natural history as continuous) draws false inferences that overlook fundamental differences between human and nonhuman beings. Nassar defends Herder's analogical method as a philosophically legitimate approach to natural history that Kant's framework cannot accommodate.

  RELEVANCE: Addresses the Kant-Herder debate about the philosophical status of the Ideen's naturalistic-providentialist method, which is essential context for assessing the coherence of Herder's claim that human history follows natural developmental patterns guided by divine providence. The methodological question -- whether analogy between nature and history is legitimate -- is directly related to whether Herder's telos of Humanitat can be grounded in natural development.

  POSITION: Defends Herder's analogical methodology against Kantian objections, situating it within a different but coherent philosophy of nature.
  },
  keywords = {Kant, natural-history, analogy, methodology, Ideen, Medium},
  abstract = {In 1785 Kant published a series of critical reviews of Johann Gottfried Herder’s Ideas for a Philosophy of the History of Humanity (1784–1785), in which he not only challenges Herder’s conception of nature but also, and more importantly, his methodology. Kant’s complaint is that by relying on analogy, Herder draws deeply mistaken conclusions that overlook fundamental differences between human and nonhuman beings. But was Kant’s critique of Herder entirely fair? And how does it compare to Kant’s own use of analogy? My claim is that Herder’s use of analogy posed a fundamental methodological challenge to Kant, a challenge he sought to meet in the years following the reviews. In so doing, however, Kant found himself in the untenable situation of, on the one hand, granting analogy greater significance, and, on the other, severely restricting its use. By tracing the shifts in Kant’s thought through the lens of analogy, I aim to show that Kant’s transformed understanding of analogy reveals a fundamental tension between his a priori “metaphysics of nature” and empirical science, a tension that fundamentally shaped the philosophies of nature after Kant.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{denby2005herder,
  author = {Denby, David},
  title = {Herder: culture, anthropology and the {E}nlightenment},
  journal = {History of the Human Sciences},
  year = {2005},
  volume = {18},
  number = {1},
  pages = {55--76},
  doi = {10.1177/0952695105051126},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Denby argues against the standard reading of Herder as a proto-relativist critic of Enlightenment universalism. Comparing Herder with the naturalist Buffon, he shows that Herder's anthropological assumptions -- that humans are characterized by flexibility in relation to their environment and by the capacity for social transmission of knowledge -- are closer to Enlightenment universalism than commonly recognized. Herder's critique of progress is internally unstable because his concept of Kultur operates as a quasi-Enlightenment category.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the consistency question: can Herder's optimism about human development toward Humanitat coexist with his critique of progress narratives? Denby's argument that the critique is unstable suggests that Herder retains more of the Enlightenment progressivist framework than he acknowledges, which bears on whether Humanitat functions as a genuine telos or a disguised progressivism.

  POSITION: Challenges sharp relativist/universalist dichotomy, arguing Herder is closer to Enlightenment anthropology than his rhetoric suggests.
  },
  keywords = {Enlightenment, anthropology, Kultur, progress, relativism, Medium},
  abstract = {The anthropological sensibility has often been seen as growing out of opposition to Enlightenment universalism. Johann Gottfried Herder (1744-1803) is often cited as an ancestor of modern cultural relativism, in which cultures exist in the plural. This article argues that Herder’s anthropology, and anthropology generally, are more closely related to Enlightenment thought than is generally considered. Herder certainly attacks Enlightenment abstraction, the arrogance of its Eurocentric historical teleology, and argues the case for a proto-hermeneutical approach which emphasizes embeddedness, horizon, the usefulness of prejudice. His suspicion of the ideology of progress and of associated theories of stadial development leads to a critique of cosmopolitanism and, particularly, of colonialism. But a comparison with a central Enlightenment figure like the natural historian Buffon (1708-88) reveals shared anthropological assumptions: human beings are characterized by the flexibility of their relationship to their environment, and by their ability to transmit and receive social knowledge. Herder’s critique of progress is thus an unstable one: culture [ Kultur ] as the process through which humanity develops can also be called Enlightenment [ Aufklärung ]. Herder’s definition of culture is much closer to a unitary Enlightenment model of civilization than is frequently suggested. Herder’s relativism is thus open to question: he holds on to certain universal criteria for transcultural judgements. This reassessment of Herder’s place in Enlightenment anthropology raises questions of contemporary relevance regarding cultural relativism on the one hand, and modernization and globalization on the other.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{wells1960herder,
  author = {Wells, G. A.},
  title = {Herder's Two Philosophies of History},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Ideas},
  year = {1960},
  volume = {21},
  number = {4},
  pages = {527--537},
  doi = {10.2307/2708100},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Wells identifies a fundamental tension between two incompatible philosophies of history in Herder's work: the early relativist philosophy of Auch eine Philosophie der Geschichte (1774), which denies the superiority of any epoch, and the later progressivist philosophy of the Ideen (1784-91), which posits Humanitat as a developmental goal toward which history genuinely advances. Wells argues these cannot be reconciled without distorting one or the other.

  RELEVANCE: This classic article formulates the central interpretive problem of this domain: can a universal normative telos coexist with the equal worth of all epochs? As the first systematic statement of the "two philosophies" tension, Wells sets the agenda for all subsequent discussion, including Bristow's (2025) proposed reconciliation. Essential for any discussion of whether Herder's optimism about Humanitat is consistent with his critique of progress narratives.

  POSITION: Holds that the tension is genuine and irresolvable, representing an inconsistency in Herder's thought.
  },
  keywords = {two-philosophies-of-history, Auch-eine-Philosophie, Ideen, progress, relativism, Medium},
  abstract = {Both Stadelmann and Meinecke consider Herder's early philosophy of history, the Auch eine Philosophie der Geschichte, written in Buckeburg in 1774, to be a greater achievement than the Ideen zur Philosophie der Geschichte der Menschheit of his Weimar period, published in four volumes between 1784 and 1791. I propose in this paper to examine the reasons they give for their preference. My justification for doing so is, first, that they are regarded as important authorities on Herder, and second, that their methods of evaluating his typify an approach to history and historians common today. Stadelmann 'considers that since Herder holds all historical phenomena to be determined by their antecedents, he is not entitled to criticize the medieval clergy and aristocracy for their behavior; but that this is what he does in the Ideen, whereas in the early Auch eine Philosophie he was much more detached and felt his way into the past instead of passing judgment on it. In the Ideen, he says, Herder's intuitive genial [kongenial] understanding for the religious and aristocratic greatness of the Middle Ages is killed by strong liberal-republican and Protestant antipathy to the aristocratic constitution that suppressed the industry and inventiveness of the artisans, and to the tyranny the clergy exercised over men's minds. He holds that the Auch eine Philosophie is free from this defect and that Herder begins to lose his detachment only after his move to Weimar.1 This is an extraordinary statement, especially as Stadelmann himself admits that the Auch eine Philosophie is a virulent attack on modern decadence-an attack effected by boosting the medieval past. Herder, he says,  plays off the Middle Ages against modern times as heart against head, achievement against morbid reflection, firm-rootedness against dissipation  (23). If the criticism of the medieval world in the Ideen constitutes a defect in that work, then the criticism of the present in the Auch eine Philosophie is a like blemish, and later in his book Stadelmann admits that this is so:  In the Biickeburg paper Herder abandons his universal-historical standpoint when he comes to speak of the present  (89). So the criterion of detachment is, after all, useless as a means of evaluating the two works, and Stadelmann sets to work to find another: for of the fact he has no doubt:  The Humanititsbriefe and even the Ideen are further removed from true historical sense than Herder's youthful works (57). The following passage is an attempt to justify this view:},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{solanki2024poesis,
  author = {Solanki, Tanvi},
  title = {Poesis, {G}od, and the Connectedness of All Beings},
  journal = {Historical Reflections/R{\'e}flexions Historiques},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {50},
  number = {1},
  pages = {1--21},
  doi = {10.3167/hrrh.2024.500101},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Solanki examines Herder's analogical thinking as emerging from his Spinozist concept of God and nature, showing that Herder's comparative method posits phenomena as instantiating a common divine-natural creative power (poesis) in their own singular manner. This method resists both assimilation of differences and static hierarchies, and was influential on Goethe. Solanki argues it has been overlooked because it resists standard historicist periodization.

  RELEVANCE: Connects Herder's theological-metaphysical framework (the Spinozist God of Gott) to his methodology for understanding cultural and natural diversity -- directly relevant to the question of how providentialism and particular-cultural worth are related. The argument that Herder's comparative method refuses hierarchy without flattening difference illuminates how the telos of Humanitat functions without imposing a ranked hierarchy of cultures.

  POSITION: Argues that Herder's analogical method, grounded in his Spinozist theology, provides a framework for non-hierarchical cross-cultural comparison.
  },
  keywords = {analogy, Spinozism, comparative-method, Gott, poesis, Medium},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
The article examines the significance of J. G. Herder's analogical thinking in the history of comparative practices. It shows how Herder's comparative method, of positing one phenomenon as another in its own singular and inimitable manner, emerges from his concept of God and nature as informed by Baruch Spinoza, as well as his study of scientific and cultural phenomena, including the active mechanisms of poesis as sensory perception. While Herder's concept of comparison and his analogical practices influenced prominent thinkers contemporary to him such as J.W. Goethe, the specificity of his comparative method has been overlooked by scholars past and present, such as Michel Foucault, as Herder's approach resists assimilation in histories of comparativism centered on nineteenth-century projects of comparison. Yet his outlook on the tension between difference and identity offers fresh insight into relations between distinct cultures and entities without effacing their particularity or placing them in static hierarchies. Herder's comparative method provides new perspectives on contemporary debates on the benefits of and dangers of historical analogies.},
}

@article{layne2023herder,
  author = {Layne, Priscilla and Norberg, Jakob},
  title = {Herder and the Black Slave},
  journal = {Monatshefte},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {115},
  number = {4},
  pages = {553--572},
  doi = {10.3368/m.115.4.553},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Layne and Norberg argue that Herder's "Negro Idylls" in the tenth collection of the Briefe zur Beförderung der Humanität are politically revealing: Herder's hope for the future redemption of humanity requires that Black slaves renounce retribution for historical wrongs, making his concept of Humanitat structurally dependent on the self-effacement of the oppressed. Despite his genuine anti-slavery commitments, Herder's universal humanity is thus "shored up" by a conception of the Black subject that denies full humanity.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a critical perspective on what Humanitat actually entails in the Briefe, Herder's most sustained late treatment of the concept. The argument that Humanitat's universalism conceals a structural exclusion is directly relevant to the question of whether a universal normative telos can coexist with genuine cultural particularity and whether Herder's providentialism is egalitarian or implicitly hierarchical.

  POSITION: Critical reading: Humanitat's universalism contains a structural contradiction when confronted with the reality of racialized oppression.
  },
  keywords = {Humanitat, Briefe, race, slavery, universalism, critique, Medium},
  abstract = {We argue that Herder’s often-ignored “Negro Idylls” in the tenth and final collection of his Briefe zu Beförderung der Humanität are both politically significant and highly revealing of the limitations of Herder’s political philosophy. The poems’ compassionate but politically confining representations of Black slaves ultimately seek to resolve the internal tensions of his own account of “humanity,” the topic of the entire tenth collection, and indeed of the entire work. Our reading shows how the “Negro Idylls” reveal that the structure of Herder’s political philosophy is shored up and rendered coherent only by a particular conception of the Black slave as a non-avenger, a figure who willingly renounces retribution for historical wrongs to facilitate an otherwise threatened future redemption of humanity. Despite Herder’s genuine commitment to the abolition of slavery and his scathing critique of colonialism, then, Herder’s hope for humanity as a whole rests on the shoulders of Black subjects, even as it deprives these subjects of their own humanity. (PL/JN)},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{beiser2011german,
  author = {Beiser, Frederick C.},
  title = {The German Historicist Tradition},
  year = {2011},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi = {10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199691555.001.0001},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Beiser traces the German historicist tradition from Herder through Droysen, Dilthey, and Weber, arguing that historicism developed as a distinctive response to naturalistic and rationalist accounts of human nature and society. The chapter on Herder situates him as the founder of historicism: his central innovation is the idea that human nature is essentially historical -- that what humans are is constituted by their historical development rather than given antecedently.

  RELEVANCE: Situates Herder's philosophy of history within the broader German intellectual tradition, clarifying what is distinctive about his historicist account of Humanitat as a developmental achievement rather than a fixed essence. Beiser's analysis of the tension between historicism and normativity -- how can history have normative direction if human nature is constituted by history? -- is directly relevant to the question of whether Humanitat can function as a genuine telos.

  POSITION: Identifies Herder as the founder of historicism, distinguishing historicist from both naturalist and rationalist philosophies of human nature.
  },
  keywords = {historicism, German-tradition, historicist, Herder, Medium},
  abstract = {This is the first history in English of German historicism, the intellectual tradition which holds that history is the key to understanding all human values, beliefs and actions. Beiser surveys the key thinkers from the mid-18th to the early 20th century and illuminates the sources and reasons for this revolution in modern thought.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{zammito2009herder,
  author = {Zammito, John},
  title = {Herder and Historical Metanarrative: What's Philosophical about History?},
  booktitle = {A Companion to the Works of {J}ohann {G}ottfried {H}erder},
  editor = {Adler, Hans and Koepke, Wulf},
  year = {2009},
  pages = {65--92},
  publisher = {Camden House},
  doi = {10.1017/9781571137289.004},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Zammito examines what makes Herder's philosophy of history genuinely philosophical, arguing that Herder attempts to construct a historical metanarrative -- an account of the overall shape and meaning of human history -- while remaining skeptical of dogmatic schemes. Zammito traces the tensions between Herder's anti-teleological rhetoric and his reliance on developmental metaphors, and assesses whether Herder's narrative can bear the philosophical weight he assigns to it.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses whether Herder's philosophy of history is internally coherent as a philosophical enterprise, particularly the question of whether a historical metanarrative that includes a normative telos (Humanitat) is compatible with Herder's anti-dogmatic, empirical, and pluralist commitments. Zammito's analysis of the metanarrative problem connects the Humanitat debate to broader questions about the status of Herder's philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Critical examination of the metanarrative ambitions of Herder's history, identifying unresolved tensions between the philosophical and the anti-systematic.
  },
  keywords = {metanarrative, philosophy-of-history, telos, anti-teleology, Medium},
  abstract = {THERE IS SOME TRUTH in Goethe's pronouncement that Johann Gottfried Herder's ideas had been both absorbed and forgotten by the conventional wisdom of German culture after 1800. He was absorbed either into the project of the Jena Romantiker and their literary hermeneutics or into the project of Hegel and his philosophy of history. But after the rise of historicism in the school of Ranke and Droysen, a retrospective redemption seemed possible. Thus, in the classic formulations of Meinecke and Stadelmann, Herder was resurrected as the "father of historicism." He was credited with pioneering the stress on individuality, development, and the "historical sense" of Einfühlung (or Verstehen [empathy]) that became the core of the disciplinary matrix of history in the nineteenth century.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{englander2013herder,
  author = {Englander, Alex},
  title = {Herder's {`E}xpressivist{'} Metaphysics and the Origins of {G}erman {I}dealism},
  journal = {British Journal for the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2013},
  volume = {21},
  number = {5},
  pages = {902--924},
  doi = {10.1080/09608788.2013.805120},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Englander examines Herder's "expressivist" metaphysics -- the idea that reality consists of forces that express themselves through natural and cultural forms -- and argues that this framework is a significant but underappreciated source for German Idealism. Herder's claim that force expresses itself through particular forms without being exhausted by any of them provides a model for understanding how a universal divine power can manifest through the irreducible particularity of cultures.

  RELEVANCE: Connects Herder's Spinozist/Kraft metaphysics to his philosophy of history: if the divine force expressing itself through history is identified with Humanitat, then expressivist metaphysics provides the ontological grounding for why universal and particular are not in tension but mutually constituting. This article clarifies how Herder's theology in Gott supports rather than undermines the pluralism of the Ideen.

  POSITION: Situates Herder's metaphysics as expressivist rather than simply Spinozist, linking it to the genesis of German Idealism.
  },
  keywords = {expressivist-metaphysics, Kraft, German-idealism, Spinozism, Low},
  abstract = {AbstractCharles Taylor's influential exposition of Hegel made the doctrine of expressivism of central importance and identified Herder as its exemplary historical advocate. The breadth and generality of Taylor's use of ‘expressivism’ have led the concept into some disrepute, but a more precise formulation of the doctrine as a theory of meaning can both demonstrate what is worthwhile and accurate in Taylor's account, and allow us a useful point of entry into Herder's multifaceted philosophy. A reconstruction of Herder's overall philosophical position, centred around a refined theory of what this paper labels ‘Herderean expressivism’, reveals a naturalistic, teleological metaphysics. This metaphysics fulfils the Hegelian aim of providing what Dieter Henrich has called a ‘feedback loop’ between ontology and epistemology. Exploring Herder's expressivism, therefore, helps further the case for his decisive impact on Hegel's philosophy. Herder's methodological naturalism, however, represents an obstacle to Hegel's absolute idealism.Keywords: J. G. HerderG. W. F. Hegelexpressivism Notes1 A helpful guide to the politics of these disputes can be found in Hacking, Historical Ontology, 121–40. Berlin, it should be noted, celebrates Herder's cosmopolitanism and humanism. Yet he believes what he calls Herder's ‘pluralism’ slides into cultural relativism and finds its ‘consequences’ in Romantic anti-rationalism and nationalism. See Berlin, Vico and Herder, 212–3.2 In this paper, I concentrate upon Hegel, although an analogous account could certainly be given for Schelling. For some details of the Herder-Schelling relation, see Beiser, German Idealism.3 There is a long scholarly tradition emphasizing the decisive influence of Herder upon Hegel. See Harris, Hegel's Development. What Hegel read of Herder is difficult to ascertain. Most importantly, we know he wrote a (now missing) review of the two editions of Herder's dialogue Gott: Einige Gespräche [henceforth God] of 1787 and 1800. Hegel refers to several of Herder's writings on the Old Testament in his early theological essays and later lectures on religion, and comments upon his philosophy in the 1802/1803 Glauben und Wissen. There are abundant signs that he was familiar with several of Herder's central works, in particular his Vom Erkennen und Empfinden der Menschlichen Seele [henceforth On the Cognition] and his pioneering work in the philosophy of history, the Ideen zur Philosophie der Geschichte der Menscheit [henceforth Ideas]. For a detailed examination, see Forster, Hegel's Idea of a Phenomenology.4 This limited reconstruction of Herder's philosophy certainly omits some of the ideas which were most relevant to his philosophical successors, especially his ‘hylozoism’ or vitalism, which was crucial both for the idealists' epistemological questions and for the development of Naturphilosophie. For these topics, see Nisbet, Herder and Scientific Thought; Zammito, ‘Epigenesis’, 131–45.5 Hacking, Why Does Language Matter, 15–43, offers a good account of how this characterization applies to Hobbes, the Port Royal Logic, Locke and Berkeley, all representatives of the ‘way of ideas’.6 This characterization of ‘crude empiricist designativism’ is indebted to Rosen. Taylor's presentation of the object of expressivist attack agrees with these particulars. He explains how pre-expressivist theories involve an ontological division between semantic and non-semantic entities, with the former gaining their semantic properties through merely ‘external relations’ of reference to the latter. These relations are established ‘subjectively’, and therefore exist in the mind alone.7 Herder, unlike certain later romantics, does not regard the coalescence of form and matter as an a priori obstacle to successful translation. His own theory of translation contains several innovative methods for recapturing the tones and senses of original works. For a helpful account of Herder's theory, see Ulrich Gaier's commentary to Herder's Volkslieder, especially W3: 909–18. References to Herder's German texts are cited by ‘W’ followed by volume and page number, and taken from Herder, Werke. Translations of Herder's writings are from Philosophical Writings, henceforth cited by ‘PW’ and page number, and Selected Writings on Aesthetics, cited by ‘Aesthetics’ and page number. Translations from Gott: Einige Gespräche are from God: Some Conversations.8 See Irmscher, ‘Beobachtungen zur Funktion der Analogie im Denken Herders’, 64–98, 65–75 for discussion.9 See Nisbet for an extended discussion.10 See Clark, ‘Herder's Conception of “Kraft”’, 737–52, for details.11 As Marion Heinz points out, sensations [Empfindungen] in Herder refer both to a system of bodily states or low-grade forces of representation (in Leibnizian fashion) and to representations of the soul on the other mediated through the body. The latter, cognitive, capacity is inseparable from the former. See Heinz, ‘Grundzüge von Herders Psychologie’, 137–52, 142.12 Cf. Herder, PW, 210–11, One will never get to bottom of these forces [i.e. mental capacities] if one merely treats them superficially as ideas that dwell in the soul, or, worse still, separates them from one another as walled compartments and considers them individually in independence.13 The many subordinate forces of the soul such as imagination, foresight and memory must not be seen as separated off from one another and as separate powers, but as at bottom a single force that is ‘understanding, intuition with inner consciousness’ or ‘inner activity that looks within itself, consciousness, apperception’ PW, 210–11.14 See Beiser, The Fate of Reason, 145ff. In the 1775 draft of On the Cognition, Herder states that ‘Psychology must at every step be physiology’. In this essay, Herder wants to base all sensation, and by extension all thought, on one purely physiological manifestation of force, Haller's discovery of ‘irritability’ [Reizbarkeit]. Psychology is equally physiology since ‘Sensation is only the aggregate of all obscure irritations, just as thought is the luminous aggregate of sensation.’ Herder, PW, 197. In the same draft, Herder explains how whilst there is a continuity of laws in sensation and cognition, they are present in the latter in a ‘higher equation’ and the ‘string-play of an irritation … now communicates itself and takes effect in an infinitely more mentalistic, higher way’ PW, 201.15 Irmscher, 86. Cf. Aesthetics 359: ‘Human cognition starts from the senses and from experience, and everything comes back to them.’16 Gaier, ‘The problem…’, 301 goes so far as to say that the notion of ‘harmonisation’ or ‘coherence’ functions as a ‘transcendental principle’ for Herder.17 In stark contrast to Hume, Herder believes that our activities of observation, abstraction, combination, and inference are, though not infallible, inner necessities of thought which derive from divine necessity. They are activities standing in analogous relation to the organizational principles governing groupings of forces throughout the cosmos. Hence, Herder states that this necessary basis of thought is to the world of minds what equilibrium is to the world of bodies. As such, they possess a qualified universality; a being with finer senses would still think according to the same unique necessary divine ‘laws’. Cf. God, 149–50 and PW, 208.18 PW, 215: ‘true cognition and good volition are just one sort of thing, a single force and efficacy of the soul.’19 See the discussion at W1:144.20 For further discussion, see Stern, ‘Kant, Hegel, and the Place of the Subject’.21 This meaning of subjectivism is at issue in the discussion of cultural absolutism versus relativism in Spencer, Herder's Political Thought, chap. 4. Spencer contends that Herder charts a sophisticated middle course she calls ‘weak pluralism’.22 This form of subjectivism, criticised earlier as an element of Taylor's expressivism, can nourish a version of (2), especially an aesthetic version: putatively ‘objective’ standards cannot do justice to the unique particularity of artworks given their role as expressions of the artist's irreducible subjectivity. Indeed, many commentators criticize this element of Taylor's interpretation precisely for its association of Herder with elements of later Romanticism. See Spencer, Herder's Political Thought, 42–3, and Dallmayr, ‘Truth and Diversity’, 101–24, 107–9. I agree with Spencer, however, that Dallmayr throws the baby out with the bathwater by rejecting the term expressivism altogether.23 Whilst correctly distancing Herder from scepticism and idealism (‘fully-fledged scepticism’), Sikka agrees that for both Kant and Herder our fundamental concepts are ‘subjective’ in being inapplicable to ‘things in themselves’. Sikka, 205.24 I thank Raymond Geuss, Michael Forster, Gabriel Roberts and two anonymous referees for the journal for their helpful comments on various versions of this essay.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}
